  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "For how many integers \\(N\\) between \\(1\\) and \\(1990\\) is the improper fraction \\[ \\frac{N^2+7}{N+4} \\] not in lowest terms?",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(86\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(90\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(104\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(105\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "b",
    "value": "86"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1990-p19.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Modulo \\(N+4,\\) we have \\(N\\equiv-4,\\) so \\[ N^2+7\\equiv16+7=23. \\] Thus the fraction is reducible exactly when \\(N+4\\) is divisible by \\(23,\\) or \\(N\\equiv19\\pmod{23}.\\) The values are \\(19,42,\\ldots,1974,\\) a total of \\(86.\\)\n\nThus the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "Reduce \\(N^2+7\\) modulo \\(N+4\\)",
        "The only possible common prime factor is a divisor of \\(23\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
